{"name":"Future Fuchsia","hex":"#ff2040","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"future","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The time ahead; those moments yet to be experienced.","Something that will happen in moments yet to come."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having to do with or occurring in the future."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/future"},{"term":"fuchsia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A popular garden plant, of the genus Fuchsia, of the Onagraceae family, shrubs with red, pink or purple flowers.","A purplish-red colour, the color of fuchsin, an aniline dye."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a purplish-red colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fuchsia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Future","blocks":[{"text":"The future is the time after the past and the present. Its arrival is considered inevitable due to the existence of time and the laws of physics. Due to the apparent nature of the reality and the unavoidability of the future, everything that currently exists and will exist can be categorized as either permanent, meaning that it will exist forever, or temporary, meaning that it will end. In the Occidental view, which uses a linear conception of time, the future is the portion of the projected timeline that is anticipated to occur. In special relativity, the future is considered absolute future, or the future light cone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Zeitpyramide_2023_B.jpg/330px-Zeitpyramide_2023_B.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Future","wikidata":"Q344"},{"title":"Fuchsia","blocks":[{"text":"Fuchsia is a genus of flowering plants that consists mostly of shrubs or small trees."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Brincos_De_Princesa.jpg/330px-Brincos_De_Princesa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuchsia","wikidata":"Q161066"},{"title":"Fuchsia (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Fuchsia is a vivid pinkish–red color, named after the color of the flower of the fuchsia plant, which was named by a French botanist, Charles Plumier, after the 16th-century German botanist Leonhart Fuchs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/DoubleFuchsias_wb.jpg/330px-DoubleFuchsias_wb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuchsia_(color)","wikidata":"Q5005364"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/MontreGousset001.jpg/500px-MontreGousset001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/MontreGousset001.jpg/500px-MontreGousset001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMontreGousset001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Stanford_Torus_interior.jpg/500px-Stanford_Torus_interior.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Stanford_Torus_interior.jpg/1280px-Stanford_Torus_interior.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Stanford_Torus_interior.jpg/500px-Stanford_Torus_interior.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Project of an orbital colony Stanford torus, painted by Donald E. Davis","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStanford_Torus_interior.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Sortie_de_l%27op%C3%A9ra_en_l%27an_2000-2.jpg/500px-Sortie_de_l%27op%C3%A9ra_en_l%27an_2000-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wik